# 22-01-26 — Project 03 — Linux Filesystem Awareness

## Summary
Today I continued building Linux fundamentals with a focus on navigating the filesystem and understanding directory structure.

I created Project 03 and practiced moving around the terminal using `cd`, `ls`, and relative paths.

## What I Practiced
- Navigating directories using `cd`
- Moving up one directory using `cd ..`
- Listing directory contents with `ls`
- Understanding where I am using `pwd`
- Creating structured project folders
- Basic Git workflow (add → commit → push)

## What Confused Me

Jumping more than one directory at a time.

I currently visualise navigation as:
- `cd ..`
- `ls`
- `cd <folder>`

When I tried using things like:
- `cd ../..`
- `cd ../../projects`

It didn’t feel intuitive.

I realised this may be coming from a Windows GUI mindset, where I visually click folders rather than thinking in terms of a tree structure.

Right now, I understand:
- `cd ..` = go up one level

But chaining multiple `..` feels abstract because I’m not fully visualising the directory tree in my head yet.

## What Clicked

- The filesystem is a tree.
- `..` simply means “parent directory”.
- Every directory has a parent except `/` (root).
- Using `pwd` constantly helps anchor my position.

## Reflection

For now, I will continue using:
- `cd ..`
- `ls`
- `pwd`

Even if it feels basic.

Understanding where I am in the filesystem is more important than moving quickly.

Speed will come after spatial awareness.

This is foundational work.
